<worksheet xml:id="PA-"><title>Introduction to Polynomials</title>
    <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li>What is a Polynomial?</li>
      <li>Adding and Subtracting Polynomials</li>
      <li>Multiplying Polynomials</li>

    </ul>
  </objectives>
<section><title>What is a Polynomial?</title>
   <definition>
    <statement>
        <p>
            A <alert>term</alert> is a single number or variable, or numbers and variables multiplied together. 
        </p>
    </statement>
   </definition>
   <example>
    <p>
        Here are some examples of terms: <md>3,x,4y,5ab,7x^2y^3</md>.
    </p>
   </example>
   <definition>
    <statement>
        <p>
           A <alert>monomial</alert> is a constant, a variable, or a product of constants and variables. A monomial has only one term.
        </p>
    </statement>
   </definition>
   <example>
    <p>
        Here are some examples of monomials: <md>3,x,4x^5,5ab,7x^2y^3</md>.
    </p>
   </example>
   <definition>
    <statement>
        <p>
           A <alert>polynomial</alert> is a sum (or difference) of monomials.
        </p>
    </statement>
   </definition>
   <example>
    <p>
        Here are some examples of polynomials: <md>3+x,4x^5-5ab,7x^2y^3,3x^2+5x-1,5x^3-4x^2-2x+1</md>.
    </p>
   </example>

   <definition>
    <statement>
        <p>
          The <alert>degree of a term</alert> is the sum of the exponents of the variables in the term. The <alert>degree of a polynomial</alert> is the degree of its term with the largest degree.  
        </p>
    </statement>
   </definition>
   <example>
    <p>
        Here are some examples of degrees of polynomials: <md>3+x</md> has degree 1, <md>4x^5-5ab</md> has degree 5, <md>7x^2y^3</md> has degree 5, <md>3x^2+5x-1</md> has degree 2, and <md>5x^3-4x^2-2x+1</md> has degree 3.
    </p>
   </example>

   <definition>
    <statement>
        <p>
            The <alert>coefficient</alert> of a term is the numerical factor of the term. 
        </p>
    </statement>
   </definition>
   <example>
    <p>
        Here are some examples of coefficients: <md>3</md> has coefficient 3, <md>x</md> has coefficient 1, <md>4x^5</md> has coefficient 4, <md>5ab</md> has coefficient 5, and <md>7x^2y^3</md> has coefficient 7.
    </p>
   </example>
   </section>

<section><title>Adding and Subtracting Polynomials</title>
    <definition>
        <statement>
            <p>
               <alert>Like terms</alert> are terms that have the same variables raised to the same powers.  
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>
    <example>
        <p>
            Here are some examples of like terms: <md>3x^2\mbox{ and } 4x^2</md> are like terms, <md>5xy\mbox{ and } -2xy</md> are like terms, and <md>7ab^2\mbox{ and } 3ab^2</md> are like terms.
        </p>
          <p>
            Here are some examples of <alert>non-</alert>like terms: <md>3x^2\mbox{ and } 4x^3</md> are not like terms, <md>5xy\mbox{ and } -2x^3y</md> are not like terms, and <md>7ab^2\mbox{ and } 3a^2b^2</md> are not like terms.
        </p>
    </example>
    <definition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                To add or subtract polynomials, combine like terms. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>
    <example>
        <p>
            For example, to add <md>3x^2 + 2x - 5</md> and <md>4x^2 - 3x + 1</md>, combine like terms: 
            <md>(3x^2 + 4x^2) + (2x - 3x) + (-5 + 1) = 7x^2 - x - 4</md>.
        </p>
    </example>
    <example>
        <p>
            For example, to subtract <md>4x^2 - 3x + 1</md> from <md>3x^2 + 2x - 5</md>, distribute the negative sign and combine like terms: 
            <md>(3x^2 - 4x^2) + (2x + 3x) + (-5 - 1) = -x^2 + 5x - 6</md>.
        </p>
    </example>
    <exercise workspace="0.3in">
        <statement>
            <p>
            Add the following polynomials: <md>2x^2 + 3x - 4\mbox{ and } 5x^3-4x^2 - 2x + 1</md>.
        </p>
        </statement>
       <solution>
        <p>
            Combine like terms: <md>(5x^3) + (2x^2 - 4x^2) + (3x - 2x) + (-4 + 1) = 5x^3 - 2x^2 + x - 3</md>.
        </p>
       </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.3in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Subtract the following polynomials: <md>5x^3-4x^2 - 2x + 1\mbox{ from } 2x^2 + 3x - 4</md>. That is, evaluate <md>(2x^2 + 3x - 4) - (5x^3-4x^2 - 2x + 1)</md>.
            </p>
        </statement>
       <solution>
        <p>
            Distribute the negative sign and combine like terms: <md>(0 - 5x^3) + (2x^2 - (-4x^2)) +(3x-(-2x)) +(-4 - 1) = -5x^3 + 6x^2 + 5x - 5</md>.
        </p>
       </solution>  
    </exercise>
    </section>
<section><title>Multiplying Polynomials</title>
   <introduction>
    <p>
        We will gradually build up to multiplying polynomials. First, we will review multiplying monomials, then we will multiply a monomial by a polynomial, and finally we will multiply two polynomials.
    </p>
   </introduction>
    <example>
       <p>
         Multiply the following monomials: <md>3x^2\mbox{ and } 4x^3</md>.
       </p>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Multiply the coefficients and add the exponents of the variables: <md>3\cdot 4=12\mbox{ and } </md><md>x^2\cdot x^3=x^5\mbox{ so }</md><md>(3x^2)\cdot(4x^3)=12x^5</md>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>
   <exercise workspace="0.3in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Multiply the following monomials: <md>5x^4\mbox{ and } -2x^3</md>.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Multiply the coefficients and add the exponents of the variables: <md>5\cdot -2=-10\mbox{ and } </md><md>x^4\cdot x^3=x^7\mbox{ so }</md><md>(5x^4)\cdot(-2x^3)=-10x^7</md>.
        </p>
    </solution> 
   </exercise>
   <example>
    <p>
        Multiply the following monomial and polynomial: <md>3x^2\mbox{ and } 4x^3-5x+2</md>.
    </p>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Distribute the monomial to each term of the polynomial: 
                <md>(3x^2)(4x^3) + (3x^2)(-5x) + (3x^2)(2) = 12x^5 - 15x^3 + 6x^2</md>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>
   <exercise workspace="0.3in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Multiply the following monomial and polynomial: <md>-2x^3\mbox{ and } 5x^2-3x+4</md>.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Distribute the monomial to each term of the polynomial:
            <md>(-2x^3)(5x^2) + (-2x^3)(-3x) + (-2x^3)(4) </md><md>= -10x^5 + 6x^4 - 8x^3</md>.
            We can also use the box method to help us organize our work. 
<tabular top="major" bottom="major" left="major" right="major" row="minor" column="minor" >
<row header="yes"><cell><md>\cdot</md></cell><cell><md>5x^2</md></cell><cell><md>-3x</md></cell><cell><md>4</md></cell></row>
<row><cell><md>-2x^3</md></cell><cell><md>-10x^5</md></cell><cell><md>6x^4  </md></cell><cell><md>-8x^3</md></cell></row>
</tabular>
        </p>
    </solution>

   </exercise>
   <example>
    <p>
        Multiply the following polynomials: <md>2x+3\mbox{ and } x^2-4x+5</md>.
    </p>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Distribute each term of the first polynomial to each term of the second polynomial:
                <md>(2x)(x^2) + (2x)(-4x) + (2x)(5) + (3)(x^2) + (3)(-4x) + (3)(5)</md><md>= 2x^3 - 8x^2 + 10x + 3x^2 - 12x + 15</md><md>= 2x^3 - 5x^2 - 2x + 15</md>.
            </p>
            <p>
                We can also use the box method to help us organize our work.    
                <tabular top="major" bottom="major" left="major" right="major" row="minor" column="minor" >
<row header="yes"><cell><md>\cdot</md></cell><cell><md>x^2</md></cell><cell><md>-4x</md></cell><cell><md>5</md></cell></row>
<row><cell><md>2x</md></cell><cell><md>2x^3</md></cell><cell><md>-8x^2</md></cell><cell><md>10x</md></cell></row>
    <row>
        <cell><md>3</md></cell>
        <cell><md>3x^2</md></cell>
        <cell><md>-12x</md></cell>
        <cell><md>15</md></cell>
    </row>
</tabular>
We next combine like terms to get <md>2x^3 - 5x^2 - 2x + 15</md>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>
 <exercise workspace="1.5in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Multiply the following polynomials: <md>x^2-4x+5\mbox{ and } x^3-x^2-10</md>.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <p>
        Use the box method to help organize your work. Then combine like terms to get your final answer.
         <tabular top="major" bottom="major" left="major" right="major" row="minor" column="minor" >
<row header="yes"><cell><md>\cdot</md></cell><cell><md>x^2\quad\quad\quad</md></cell><cell><md>-4x\quad\quad\quad</md></cell><cell><md>5\quad\quad\quad</md></cell></row>

<row><cell><md>x^3</md></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    </row>
    <row>
        <cell><md>-x^2</md></cell>
        <cell></cell>
        <cell></cell>
        <cell></cell>
    </row>
    <row>
        <cell><md>-10</md></cell>
        <cell></cell>
       <cell></cell>
        <cell></cell>
    </row>
</tabular>
    </p>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Distribute each term of the first polynomial to each term of the second polynomial:
            <md>(x^2)(x^3) + (x^2)(-x^2) + (x^2)(-10) + (-4x)(x^3) + (-4x)(-x^2) + (-4x)(-10) + (5)(x^3) + (5)(-x^2) + (5)(-10)</md>
            <md>= x^5 - x^4 - 10x^2 - 4x^4 + 4x^3 + 40x + 5x^3 - 5x^2 - 50</md>
            <md>= x^5 - 5x^4 + 9x^3 - 15x^2 + 40x - 50  </md>.
        </p>
        <p>
            We can also use the box method to help us organize our work.    
            <tabular top="major" bottom="major" left="major" right="major" row="minor" column="minor" >
<row header="yes"><cell><md>\cdot</md></cell><cell><md>x^2</md></cell><cell><md>-4x</md></cell><cell><md>5</md></cell></row>
<row><cell><md>x^3</md></cell><cell><md>x^5</md></cell><cell><md>-4x^4</md></cell><cell><md>5x^3</md></cell></row>
    <row>
        <cell><md>-x^2</md></cell>
        <cell><md>-x^4</md></cell>
        <cell><md>4x^3</md></cell>
        <cell><md>-5x^2</md></cell>
    </row>
    <row>
        <cell><md>-10</md></cell>
        <cell><md>-10x^2</md></cell>
        <cell><md>40x</md></cell>
        <cell><md>-50</md></cell>
    </row>
</tabular>
   We next combine like terms to get <md>x^5 - 5x^4 + 9x^3 - 15x^2 + 40x - 50</md>.
        </p>
    </solution>
 </exercise>
 <exercise>
 <statement>
    <p>
    Multiply the following polynomials, and simplify your answer:
 </p>
 </statement>
 <task workspace="1.0in">
    <statement>
        <p>
           <m>(x-3)(x-5)</m> 
        </p>
    </statement>
 </task>

 <task workspace="1.0in">
    <statement>
        <p>
           <m>(2x-5)(2x+5)</m> 
        </p>
    </statement>
 </task>

 <task workspace="1.0in">
    <statement>
        <p>
           <m>(2x-5)^2</m> 
        </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
        <p>
           <m>(2x-5)^2</m> is the same as <m>(2x-5)(2x-5)</m>. Use the distributive property (or box method)to multiply these two polynomials. 
        </p>
    </hint>
 </task>

 <task workspace="1.0in">
    <statement>
        <p>
           <m>-3(2x-1)(-4x+5)</m> 
        </p>
    </statement>
 </task>
 </exercise>
    </section>
  </worksheet>